<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1080">1080</a>–<a href="/years/1169">1169</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati was an Andalusian Muslim traveller born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who journeyed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the twelfth century. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Saqsin on the lower Volga and travelled on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and his writings describe the peoples, trade and marvels of these lands. His accounts are a valuable source for the Volga region before the Mongol conquest.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Hamid al-Gharnati, in full Abu Hamid Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman ibn Rabi al-Mazini al-Qaysi, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1080. His nisbas al-Mazini and al-Qaysi link him to Arab tribal lineages of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> confederation. His family is thought to have fled Granada to escape the rule of the Almoravid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a>. His kunya, Abu Hamid, means father of Hamid, after his first son, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1106 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1115 by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where he described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a>. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1123. In 1130 he visited Abhar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, where the emir received him and took lessons in Islamic law from him. The next year he lived in Saqsin on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where local experts in jurisprudence brought him difficult legal cases.</p><p>From 1131 to 1150 he lived among the Volga Bulgarians, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> he travelled to the Rus', noting the use of fur as money. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> who spoke a Turkic language, and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> for the Muslims there.</p><p>He went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, where he served as an adviser at the court of King Géza II and dealt with the Muslim Pechenegs who formed an important part of the king's army. There he bought two enslaved girls, one of them a Byzantine child captured in the Hungarian wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. He left behind in Hungary an older son, established in the local Muslim community. He returned through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1154, then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> from 1161, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from 1165 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from 1169.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Al-Gharnati wrote of his journeys in the Arabic travelogue Tuhfat al-Albab, the Gift of Hearts, and of spectacular places and things in al-Mu'rib an ba'd aja'ib al-Maghrib, Praise of Some of the Wonders of the West, which also tells of his time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. These works combine geographical description, observation of the peoples he met and accounts of marvels, and they established the genre of books of wonders in Arabic. Many of the things he saw and reported are embellished with fantastic details.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Hamid al-Gharnati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the earliest introductions of Islam into Eastern Europe came through an early 11th-century Muslim prisoner of the Byzantines who was brought among the Pechenegs and converted some of them, and in the late 12th century <b>Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</b> wrote of Hungarian Pechenegs, probably Muslims, living disguised as Christians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…travellers left important descriptions of it: Ibn Fadlan’s account of his journey, including his description of a Rus funeral on the Volga, is one of the main sources for the early history of eastern Europe, and the Andalusian traveller <b>Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</b> visited Bolghar in the twelfth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1137" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Hamid al-Gharnati"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4755 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4755 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1137">What was here in 1137? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
